{"data":{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.23604860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.5281/zenodo.23604860","prefix":"10.5281","suffix":"zenodo.23604860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"oai:zenodo.org:23604860","identifierType":"oai"}],"alternateIdentifiers":[{"alternateIdentifierType":"oai","alternateIdentifier":"oai:zenodo.org:23604860"}],"creators":[{"name":"Carroll, Jeremy H.","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy H.","familyName":"Carroll","nameIdentifiers":[],"affiliation":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conditional Density Lift and Dual-Witness Xi Functional"},{"title":"A finite mathematical construction with explicit evidence boundaries","titleType":"Subtitle"}],"publisher":"Zenodo","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"cohomology"},{"subject":"primal-dual optimization"},{"subject":"finite cochain complex"},{"subject":"gauge invariance"},{"subject":"topological data mechanics"},{"subject":"quantum information correspondence"},{"subject":"primal residual"},{"subject":"dual circulation"},{"subject":"operator-valued lift"},{"subject":"finite descent authority"},{"subject":"deterministic tie-breaking"},{"subject":"LP duality"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-10-23","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":"en","types":{"ris":"GEN","bibtex":"misc","citeproc":"article","schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceType":"","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.20192296","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"v2.0.5 Pre-Print","rightsList":[{"rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0","rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX"},{"rights":"Copyright (C) 2026 Jeremy H. Carroll, Author","rightsUri":"http://rightsstatements.org/vocab/InC/1.0/"}],"descriptions":[{"description":"Abstract\n\nA nonzero real vector can be mapped to a unit vector by taking signed square roots of its normalized absolute coordinates. The associated rank-one projector retains relative magnitudes and signs while forgetting total mass and common sign. We apply this construction to an optimal residual and an optimal dual witness for a finite-dimensional minimum-distance problem in the sum norm. Their trace distance defines an alignment statistic. Complementary slackness gives a strict upper bound below one for every optimal pair, and characterizes the zero-distance case. Successive coordinate minimization selects a unique pair once a coordinate order is fixed. The construction is undefined at a zero residual; a separate convention assigns the statistic the value zero there. We give proofs, an explicit example, and a selection policy that uses the statistic only after primary residual comparisons. The accompanying numerical implementation and discrete Lean results have distinct, stated scopes.\n\nWhy This Matters\n\nThe paper distinguishes what its diagnostic inequalities measure from stronger claims that do not follow. Explicit definitions and counterexamples make the selection policy and its limits inspectable.\n\nCore Theorems\n\nThe numbered propositions and proofs in the manuscript state their domains and hypotheses. The revision separates quotient residuals, input norms, discrete formal lemmas and numerical evaluations rather than treating them as equivalent.\n\nFormal Verification and Computational Artifacts\n\nThe package includes source code, tests, a pinned standalone Lean project, an explicit declaration census and source-bound rendered files. The quick start gives commands for replay. Each formal result retains the scope described in the manuscript; test outcomes do not supply a theorem beyond that scope.\n\nScope Boundaries and Non-Claims\n\nThe real projector construction and strict primal-dual distance bound have prose proofs. Lean checks discrete integer and rational statements. Numerical vector evaluation does not establish optimality or compute the canonical selector.\n\nReproduction\n\nSee [quick_start.md](quick_start.md) for installation, tests, the Lean census, rendering and independent archive checks. The manuscript and documentation use CC-BY-4.0; reference software and tests use MIT. The renderer, Lua filter and path helperretain Apache-2.0; see the component table in README.md.\n\nRelated Identifiers and Citations\n\nVerified scholarly sources are listed in [references.md](references.md).The new revision has no assigned DOI. The previous record is [10.5281/zenodo.20192297](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20192297); its verified parent is [10.5281/zenodo.20192296](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20192296). The metadata records these version relationships.","descriptionType":"Abstract"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"xml":"","url":"https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.23604860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"api","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"viewsOverTime":[],"downloadCount":0,"downloadsOverTime":[],"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"citationsOverTime":[],"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-10-23T21:19:16.000Z","registered":"2026-10-23T21:19:17.000Z","published":"2026","updated":"2026-10-23T21:19:17.000Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"cern.zenodo","type":"clients"}},"provider":{"data":{"id":"cern","type":"providers"}},"media":{"data":{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.23604860","type":"media"}},"references":{"data":[]},"citations":{"data":[]},"parts":{"data":[]},"partOf":{"data":[]},"versions":{"data":[]},"versionOf":{"data":[{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.20192296","type":"dois"}]}}}}